# Hold the twin's current mode for one run, or release that hold (ADR-0072, S-01).
#
# A client that checks the twin's mode and then sends a command races every other
# client: between its check and the boundary's dispatch another client may switch
# the twin, and the command then reaches sides that are not the run's target - in
# the worst case a SIM run's next command reaching the physical arm. A hold closes
# that race IN THE BOUNDARY: while one exists, SetMode into any mode other than the
# held one is refused unless it carries the holder's id (SetMode.holder), so the
# mode a run checked is the mode every one of its goals, track moves and belt
# commands is routed in until the holder itself changes it or lets go.
#
# ACQUIRE takes the hold, and is accepted only if `mode` is the mode in force now
# (a client holds what it checked, never what it hoped for) and no other holder
# has one. Asked again by the holder it is a re-assertion, under the same rule.
# RELEASE lets go, and is accepted only from the holder (or when nothing is held).
# A transition the holder makes carries the hold to the new mode: a run that holds
# REAL and returns to SIM holds SIM until it releases.
#
# LIVENESS. The hold names the holder's ROS node (`node`, its fully qualified
# name). The boundary reads the plant's ROS graph at its heartbeat period, and a
# hold whose node has not been on that graph for the boundary's ceiling
# (`cite_twin.hold.UNSEEN_CEILING_S`) LAPSES, so a client that died - a console
# killed, a terminal closed - cannot lock the twin forever. A node that crashed
# leaves the graph only once its middleware lease expires, so that is added to
# the bound. A lapse changes NO mode: the twin stays where the dead holder left
# it, the lapse is logged, and any client may then change the mode under the
# ordinary gates (the hardware opt-in, readiness, the in-flight refusal). The
# physical side's own deadman is unaffected by a hold either way.
#
# What this is NOT: ownership of the commands themselves. A goal or a track
# command is routed by the mode whoever sends it; the hold fixes the mode, not who
# may command in it (open-work #100).
#
# SERVICE is the one place this name is written, as SetMode's is.

string SERVICE="/cite/twin/hold_mode"

uint8 ACQUIRE=1       # take, or re-assert, the hold on `mode`
uint8 RELEASE=2       # let the hold go

uint8 action          # ACQUIRE or RELEASE; 0, or any other value, is refused
string holder         # the run's own id, unique per run; never empty. SetMode.holder
                      # carries the same string
string node           # the holder's ROS node, fully qualified (`/fixed_program`): what
                      # the boundary looks for on the graph. Required with ACQUIRE
uint8 mode            # ACQUIRE: the TwinMode.MODE_* the client read and holds. Ignored
                      # with RELEASE
---
bool accepted
ResultCode result     # SUCCESS, or why not (PRECONDITION_FAILED: held by another, the
                      # mode is not the one in force, a field missing)
string holder         # who holds the twin after this call; empty when nobody does
uint8 mode            # the mode in force after this call
